  "decision": "map_front",
  "rationale": "The feasible front is very narrow: only 3 designs, all pipelined_m with data_width pinned at 19, spanning luts_plus_ffs 1417..1884 against an HV reference of 4000 and accuracy_bits 12.4..14.6 against a reference of 12. It covers only a small slice of the area objective's range and none of the data_width range (registry 8..28), so hypervolume is leaving large regions unmapped. Meanwhile pipelined alone produced 21 feasible designs (best luts_plus_ffs=1772, best accuracy 14.53 bits) yet contributes nothing to the front, and pipelined_m had only 4 feasible of 55 evals — both families clearly have unexplored feasible territory (e.g. smaller data_width with more n_iter could be cheaper while still meeting max_abs_err<=2^-12). With 100 evals and 3 rounds remaining, spending this round on a seeded NSGA-II over the full ranges of the two families already on the front (pipelined, pipelined_m) is the right way to widen coverage of the trade-off curve before any refinement. Constraints are not the blocker: throughput (best 282 vs 32) and sys_p99_batch_us (best 0.158 vs 0.44) have ample margin, and accuracy is reachable (best 2^-14.61), so the front is feasible and worth mapping, not infeasible."
  ,"next_plan": null
