{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "Budget is exhausted (200/200 evaluations, round 3 of 4 complete), so this is the final round and only `stop` or `infeasible` is valid. The design is clearly feasible, not infeasible: 88 of 200 evaluations (78 unique) satisfy all three constraints, with 0% throughput violations (best 282 MSPS vs the 32 MSPS requirement), 32% error violations but a best max_abs_err of 2.86e-08 (2^-25.06, far inside the 2^-12 limit), and 28% batch-latency violations with a best sys_p99_batch_us of 0.158 us against the 0.44 us bound. The feasible Pareto front holds 20 designs spanning luts_plus_ffs 1337..4008 (HV reference 4000) and accuracy_bits 12.3..22.7 (HV reference 12), i.e. it already covers both objective ranges essentially end-to-end, and hypervolume reached 1.787e+04 with a +28.9% gain this round. The selection rule (min luts_plus_ffs) is served by the front's cheapest point, pipelined_m [data_width=18 n_iter=16 angle_guard=-1 frac_guard=0 rounding=round m=3] at luts_plus_ffs=1337, accuracy_bits=12.3 (max_abs_err=0.000192 <= 0.000244141) and throughput 124 MSPS >= 32, with sys_p99_batch_us within the 0.44 us bound. Both families on the front (pipelined, pipelined_m) were explored, and no unexplored family remains, so further rounds would not add coverage. Caveat for the L2 stage: the cheapest front points sit closest to the error and batch-latency limits, so the L2 system simulation may drop them and re-select a slightly larger design from the same front; the front contains ample alternatives (e.g. 1564 and 1833 luts_plus_ffs points with 14.7-15.5 accuracy bits) if that happens."
